    <text type="letter">
        <body>
            <div type="writingSession">
                <pb n="1"/>
                <opener>
                    <dateline><date when="1947-04-14">April 14, 1947</date></dateline><salute>Dear
                            <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__4952" type="person">Dean
                        Schoolcraft</rs>:</salute>
                </opener>
                <p>Please excuse the delay of my answer to <rs ref="L11059" type="letter">your
                        letter of March 19th</rs> – I have been on a short Easter vacation.</p>
                <p>I am happy to recommend <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__4724" type="person">Dr.
                        Emile Kauder</rs>. <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__4724" type="person">Dr.
                        Kauder</rs> received a thorough training in economics as well as in theology
                    and history. In <rs type="place" ref="#tillich_place_id__736">Europe</rs>, <rs
                        ref="#tillich_person_id__4724" type="person">Dr. Kauder</rs> worked and
                    taught mainly in economics. After his arrival in this country, he had a small
                    research job at <rs type="place" ref="#tillich_place_id__319">New York</rs>
                    University in economics, but from 1939 on, he has been teaching languages, and
                    within the past year ancient and modern history and introduction to philosophy.
                    The fact that he has taught languages until now, rather than his proper field,
                    economics, is due to the fact that he needed some years of adjustment after
                    coming to this country. His studies in theology have been so thorough that he
                    would fit very well in your college. His last papers have been published in the
                    Anglican Theological Review.<note type="ea">Vgl. <rs
                            ref="#tillich_person_id__4724" type="person">Emil Kauder</rs>: <q><rs
                                type="work" ref="#tillich__PIFTF5XH">The development of the cycle
                                theory</rs></q> (1946); ders.: <q><rs type="work"
                                ref="#tillich__C5UTEQ4H">Ideal history: remarks on the foundation of
                                wave theory</rs></q> (1946).</note></p>
                <p>I shall be happy to <sic>gove</sic> you any additional information you may
                    wish.</p>
                <closer>
                    <salute>Sincerely yours,</salute>
                    <signed><rs ref="#tillich_person_id__1928" type="person">Prof. Paul
                        Tillich</rs></signed>
                </closer>

            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
